    {
      "paper_id": "doi:10.1007/s10640-026-01100-6",
      "sequence": 5,
      "source_sequence": 48,
      "article_type": "research-article",
      "title_en": "Catastrophic Wildfires & Suicide",
      "title_cn": "灾难性野火与自杀",
      "authors": [
        "Chandler Hubbard",
        "Nino Abashidze",
        "Hannah Hennighausen",
        "Alexander James"
      ],
      "abstract_en": "We estimate the impact of catastrophic wildfires on suicide. Leveraging an eight-year panel of county-by-month restricted-use mortality data for California, we find that catastrophic wildfires that destroy at least 2% of a county’s housing stock cause a 47% increase in the suicide rate (roughly nine excess suicides) in the six-month aftermath of an event. Exploring sources of heterogeneity, effects are largely driven by men. Mechanisms are explored and suggest that suicide results from the loss of property and direct loss of life, rather than smoke exposure. These findings suggest that wildfires may be deadlier and more expensive than previously thought.",
      "abstract_cn": "我们估计了灾难性野火对自杀的影响。利用加利福尼亚州按县-月划分的八年限制使用死亡率数据面板，我们发现，摧毁一个县至少2%住房存量的灾难性野火，在事件发生后的六个月内导致自杀率上升47%（约额外九起自杀）。在探讨异质性来源时，影响主要由男性驱动。我们探索了机制，结果表明自杀源于财产损失和直接生命损失，而非烟雾暴露。这些发现表明，野火可能比之前认为的更致命、代价更高。",
